Template: sysible/tools
Type: multiselect
Choices: terraform (BUSL, not open source), vault (BUSL, not open source), consul (BUSL, not open source), nomad (BUSL, not open source), packer (BUSL, not open source), boundary (BUSL, not open source), aws-cli (Amazon's own channel)
Default:
Description: Extra software to install on first boot
 Tick nothing to install nothing. None of this is bundled in the image, which
 is why it is offered rather than assumed.
 .
 The six HashiCorp tools are BUSL-1.1 licensed and NOT open source -- ticking
 one means accepting that licence (https://www.hashicorp.com/bsl). That is why
 each line says so, instead of one warning above a list of bare names.
 .
 You probably do not need terraform: OpenTofu, the open drop-in, is already
 installed. So are gcloud, the Azure CLI, Docker, kubectl, Helm and Ansible.
 .
 Whatever you tick is installed on the first boot with a working network, and
 can be changed afterwards with: sudo sysible-tools -- the same checklist.

Template: sysible/apps
Type: multiselect
Choices: slop (all three behind one gateway), controller (standalone), slep (standalone), connect (standalone)
Default: slop (all three behind one gateway)
Description: Sysible software to set up on first boot
 Tick what this server should run. Reported as "it was all or nothing": this
 used to be a single-choice list, so the only way to get two apps was to take
 every app. It is a checklist now, like the tools question above it.
 .
 "slop" is the whole platform behind one front door at https://slop.lan/ with a
 single sign-in. The other three are the same apps STANDALONE -- each on its own
 address, with no gateway and no shared login.
 .
 The two cannot be mixed on one machine, which is why ticking slop is what
 counts: the gateway runs those same containers under those same names, so a
 standalone copy beside it would collide on both the container name and the
 port. Tick slop for the platform, or tick the standalone apps you want.
 .
 Tick nothing to set nothing up. Any of this can be changed afterwards with:
 sudo install-sysible -- the same four choices, as a checklist there too.
 .
 Whatever you pick is installed on the first boot with a working network: the
 source is cloned from GitHub and the containers are built from Docker Hub, so
 it needs both and it is not quick. Nothing listens on the network until it
 completes.
